from pathlib import Path
import json,re
out=Path('content/isc2-cc-2026/sources/domain-5/production'); out.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
audit=Path('docs/plans/isc2-cc-2026/domain-5-audit-academic-findings.md').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
refs={}
for key,title,url,location in re.findall(r'^\| (R\d+) \| \[([^]]+)\]\(([^)]+)\), ([^|]+) \|',audit,re.M): refs[key]={'title':title,'url':url,'location':location.strip(),'checked':'2026-11-03','class':'official-scope' if key=='R01' else 'official-context' if key=='R02' else 'technical-primary'}
refs['R08']={'title':'NIST Post-Quantum Cryptography','url':'https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/post-quantum-cryptography','location':'overview, PQC standards and migration','checked':'2026-11-03','class':'technical-primary'}
refs.update({'R21':{'title':'NIST SP800-61r3 detailed profile','url':'https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/specialpublications/nist.sp.800-61r3.pdf','location':'RS.MA-02/03, RS.AN-06/07 pp27-29; RS.CO pp30-31; RC.RP recovery','checked':'2026-11-03','class':'technical-primary'},'R22':{'title':'NIST SP800-53r5','url':'https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-53r5.pdf','location':'MP-3 media marking; AU-9 audit protection; CM-2/3 configuration/change; SI-12 data minimization','checked':'2026-11-03','class':'technical-primary'},'R23':{'title':'NIST SP800-92','url':'https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/92/final','location':'log management overview; collection, analysis, storage and disposal','checked':'2026-11-03','class':'technical-primary'},'R24':{'title':'CISA Insider Threat Mitigation Guide','url':'https://www.cisa.gov/sites/default/files/2022-11/Insider%20Threat%20Mitigation%20Guide_Final_508.pdf','location':'defining insider threats; intentional and unintentional','checked':'2026-11-03','class':'technical-primary'},'R25':{'title':'ISC2 public Knowledge Checks','url':'https://www.isc2.org/certifications/quizzes','location':'public landing page; embedded actual questions not exposed by text retrieval','checked':'2026-11-03','class':'style-access-limitation'}})
rows='''5.1|Classification and labeling|Classification assigns a category under the organization’s sensitivity scheme. Labeling communicates that category through a marking or metadata; neither operation by itself enforces access.|Supply the scheme and handling rule; category names are not universal.|Label changes sensitivity; confidential always means all employees.|R01,R22|T:7-19; I03,I04
5.1|Masking and approved use|Masking hides or replaces selected values while retaining information needed for an authorized purpose. It does not inherently anonymize a dataset or authorize disclosure to another service.|State exactly which fields may be shared and with whom; public AI use is governed by policy.|Last four digits authenticate; masked records always anonymous.|R02,R22|T:19-23; I05
5.1|Sanitization and cryptographic erase|Choose sanitization for the medium, sensitivity and reuse plan; ordinary deletion is not equivalent. Cryptographic erase depends on sound prior encryption and eliminating recoverable relevant key copies, followed by verification/validation.|Do not equate SSD with one mandatory method or physical damage with assured destruction; consider backups and escrow.|Deleting a file or public key suffices; any damaged media is sanitized.|R03|T:23-27; I06
5.1|Cryptographic functions and key roles|Symmetric encryption is efficient for bulk protection. In public-key encryption the recipient’s public key protects content for the recipient’s private key; a signature is generated with the signer’s private key and verified with the matching trusted public key.|Encryption alone does not imply integrity/authenticity. Signing is not generically private-key encryption. TLS versions differ.|Signer verification hides content; sharing private keys solves distribution.|R04,R05,R17|T:27-55; I07,I11-I15
5.1|Trustworthy digest comparison|A digest comparison can show whether a file matches a trusted reference; it does not by itself identify the person who changed it. Collision resistance means finding distinct matching inputs is infeasible, not that collisions cannot exist.|Reference digest must be authenticated; MD5 unsuitable when collision resistance is required; passwords need dedicated salted costed hashing.|Hashing is reversible encryption; collision impossible; fast unsalted hash is sufficient for passwords.|R19,R18,R07|N:90-102; S:101-140; I19-I22
5.1|Post-quantum foundations|Quantum-resistant cryptography addresses attacks from quantum-capable adversaries against vulnerable cryptographic schemes. It includes key establishment and signature approaches usable on conventional computers.|Inventory vulnerable public-key dependencies, evaluate approved replacements and test migration; no prediction of quantum arrival or universal deadline.|PQC requires quantum hardware; changing bulk AES alone replaces vulnerable public-key dependencies.|R08,R01|External supplementation for missing local unit (F07)
5.2|Logging, monitoring and aggregation|Logging records occurrences; aggregation collects records from multiple sources; monitoring evaluates observations. A central repository without usable data or analysis is not assurance of detection.|Choose sources relevant to the question; protect timestamp/context and records; retain under policy, not an invented universal duration.|Collecting logs means an analyst reviewed them; SIEM replaces every endpoint defense.|R23,R22|T:79-109; I28-I33
5.2|Risk-aware triage|Triage validates reports and prioritizes investigation or action using scope, likely impact, time sensitivity and resources. Organizational escalation and response authority determine permitted next actions.|State stage, evidence, impact and procedure; no universal P1 matrix or first action.|Largest alert count first; automated label overrides business context.|R21|T:109-125; I34-I36
5.2|Signals and actual incidents|An event is an occurrence; an alert signals a condition to evaluate; an incident meets applicable incident criteria. A false positive is a benign condition flagged under the detector’s rule; a false negative is a relevant condition missed.|State ground truth and incident criteria. An incident need not wait for completed damage.|No alert means no threat; every alert proves breach.|R21,R23|N:225-255; T:119-121
5.2|Correlation and bounded inference|Relate observations using relevant host, identity and time context to assess a possible pattern. Several correlated records may strengthen an inference without proving attribution or success.|Distinguish prevented routine attempts from evidence requiring human response; validate AI-derived explanations against source records.|Correlation alone is proof; a routine block is automatically top priority.|R21,R02|I35,I37; N:275-295
5.2|Actor motives and insider risk|Actor categories suggest typical motivations, not certain identity from one technique. Insider threats can be intentional or unintentional; a deliberate theft should be distinguished from an accidental disclosure.|State intent and observed motivation explicitly; avoid assigning a nation-state from one indicator.|All insiders malicious; motive uniquely proves attribution.|R24,R15|I38,I39; T:125-129
5.2|Contextual threat intelligence|CTI is analyzed threat information useful to a decision. Indicators such as addresses/hashes guide searches, but require time, relevance and confidence checks before disruptive action.|A shared or reassigned address can reduce confidence; preserve information-sharing constraints.|A feed entry is proof of compromise; indicators remain reliable forever.|R15,R21|I40,I41; N:317-346
5.2|Adversary behavior frameworks|ATT&CK organizes observed adversary behaviors. Tactics express goals; techniques express methods. Mapping a behavior can support detection-gap analysis; the knowledge base does not block attacks itself.|No exhaustive tactic list or technique-ID recall; frameworks are aids rather than certainty.|ATT&CK is SIEM; technique is motivation.|R09|I42-I44; T:133-137
5.3|Policy-governed incident data|Protect incident records’ confidentiality, integrity and provenance; restrict access to authorized personnel. Collect/retain data under evidence procedures and retention policy; share via designated channels with approved recipients.|Apply the policy supplied in the item; respect an explicit preservation hold. Formal forensic chain of custody is not claimed mandatory for every incident.|IR permits unrestricted sharing; routine deletion overrides a hold; public tools can receive raw records.|R21,R22|N:406,414,426,471; supplemented from RS.AN-06/07
5.3|Authorized containment|Containment limits ongoing harm or spread. An authorized responder may isolate a system or restrict a compromised session under the playbook, balancing safety, evidence and business constraints.|Specify confirmed conditions, granted authority and safety constraints. No universal report/isolate/preserve-first rule.|Wait for perfect attribution during authorized urgent containment; destroy artifacts to contain.|R21|I46,I48; N:410-426
5.3|Eradication, recovery and learning|Eradication addresses malicious components and causes. Recovery restores verified safe operation against recovery criteria; lessons learned feed improvements into controls, plans and training.|Restoring a copy is not proof persistence was removed. Stage labels are teaching aids, not a mandated NIST six-step taxonomy.|Restoration fixes every cause; review replaces remediation.|R21|N:428-450; I48,I49
5.3|Response exercises|A tabletop discusses a simulated situation to test roles/decisions. Functional technical tests exercise actual capabilities in a controlled scope, such as isolating a test endpoint or routing an escalation.|Match the exercise to the capability; no changes to live systems are implied by discussion alone.|Successful discussion proves technical function; written plan guarantees readiness.|R21,R14|I49-I51; N:491-523
5.4|Asset lifecycle accountability|Protect assets through planning, acquisition, deployment, maintenance, monitoring and secure retirement. Inventory and ownership support these activities; retirement from service is distinct from sanitizing/disposal.|Illustrative cycle labels/counts vary; final storage handling must be verified before release.|Purchase inventory covers lifecycle; powered off means safe disposal.|R22,R11,R03|I53,I54; N:531-559
5.4|Unsupported assets and residual risk|If the vendor no longer supplies security fixes, new vulnerabilities may remain unpatched. If immediate replacement is infeasible, documented restricted access/segmentation/monitoring can reduce exposure while migration proceeds.|Use stated support facts rather than product-name assumptions. Temporary controls neither restore vendor support nor remove the flaw.|Working device has no lifecycle risk; isolation makes unsupported software supported.|R11,R16|I55,I56; N:567-587
5.4|Approved state versus observed drift|Configuration management maintains approved system state and evaluates deviations. A baseline provides the reference; an approved exception or unrecorded legitimate change must be considered before declaring malice.|NIST800-128 is general system configuration guidance, not just a device benchmark. Reconcile evidence with authorization.|Every deviation is malicious; change request alone updates actual settings.|R11,R22|I57,I58,I64; N:595-625
5.4|Governed changes and verification|Change control evaluates risk and authorizes implementation under the organization’s process. Patches require appropriate assessment/testing and verification of intended installation and service operation.|State completed steps and rollback/exception authority when asking NEXT; do not derive universal order from slide arrows.|Automated deployment bypasses approval; install status proves operational success.|R11,R22|I59-I64; N:627-690
5.5|Readiness team purposes|Blue activities defend/detect/respond; red exercises simulate adversary behavior within authorization. Purple collaboration uses attack observations and defensive results to improve and retest detection.|Authorization and test scope are explicit. Purple is a collaboration approach and need not be a separate permanent team.|Red is unauthorized attack; finding a gap completes improvement without retest.|R14,R01|I66-I69; N:704-736
5.5|Scanning evidence and limits|A vulnerability scan identifies potential weaknesses, including vulnerable versions and configurations. Findings require validation and scope-aware prioritization; absence of findings does not prove absence of vulnerabilities.|Do not equate version inference with demonstrated exploitation. Testing cannot guarantee complete security.|A scanner proves every exploit succeeds; a clean scan proves secure design.|R14|I70; N:744-757
5.5|Static and dynamic testing|SAST inspects code artifacts without executing the application; DAST interacts with a running application. Both can contribute evidence about overlapping flaw classes.|Select by artifact/runtime access and testing objective, not an assertion that the tester cannot possess source.|Only one method can find injection; DAST defined by inability to know code.|R12,R20|I71-I73; N:761-793
5.5|Threat modeling through change|Threat modeling identifies valued assets, potential threats, paths and mitigations in system context. Start early and revisit when designs or assumptions change.|No mandatory named method or one-time-only claim. It complements code/runtime testing.|One design review guarantees security; scans replace design reasoning.|R13|I74; N:797-811
5.5|Physical and social test boundaries|Physical testing evaluates authorized attempts to cross access boundaries. Tailgating follows an authorized person; impersonation uses false identity; phishing uses deceptive communication. An event may combine them.|Ask about a specific action or supplied verification procedure; do not force mutually exclusive categories or call all phishing physical.|A badge stops every follower; impersonation and tailgating cannot coexist.|R14,R01|I75-I78; N:819-861'''.splitlines()
knowledge=[]
for n,row in enumerate(rows,1):
 objective,term,meaning,conditions,misconceptions,reference,source=row.split('|')
 knowledge.append({'id':f'KNOW-D5-{n:03}','family':f'AT{n:02}','objective':objective,'term':term,'explanation':meaning,'conditions':conditions,'misconceptions':misconceptions,'references':reference.split(','),'source':source,'confidence':'high for stated introductory claim; organizational requirements supplied in items','status':'CURATED'})
print('Foundations',len(knowledge),'missing references',set(k for r in knowledge for k in r['references'])-set(refs)); assert len(knowledge)==26 and all(k in refs for r in knowledge for k in r['references'])
(out/'knowledge.json').write_text(json.dumps({'references':refs,'concepts':knowledge},ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
md='# Domain 5 curated knowledge foundation\n\nDate2026-11-03. Separate from unchanged original sources and historical audit. Scope authority R01; external references verify facts, not exam frequency. English explanations below are curated reviewer synthesis, not verbatim standards. High confidence concerns limited introductory claims, not native-Thai or independent human sign-off.\n\n'
for k in knowledge: md+=f"## {k['id']} — {k['term']}\n\n**Objective:** {k['objective']}; **family:** {k['family']}; **status:** CURATED.\n\n{k['explanation']}\n\n**Conditions / scope limits:** {k['conditions']}\n\n**Misconceptions / assessed distinctions:** {k['misconceptions']}\n\n**Local evidence:** {k['source']}. **Primary verification:** {', '.join(k['references'])}.\n\n"
md+='## Primary references\n\n'+''.join(f"- **{id}** [{v['title']}]({v['url']}), {v['location']}; checked{v['checked']}; {v['class']}.\n" for id,v in refs.items())
Path('content/isc2-cc-2026/knowledge/domain-5-curated.md').write_text(md,encoding='utf-8')
# Preserve original issue text and add explicit disposition, foundation and qualifiers.
ledger='# Domain 5 academic remediation ledger\n\nDate2026-11-03. All21 historical findings retained below. Source locations/wording are in the immutable audit and originals. Resolutions apply to the new curated foundation, not retroactive repairs to course material. Confidence high for the limited claim or exclusion. F19 provenance/cropping limitations remain disclosed, nonblocking because no unseen text is relied upon.\n\n'
mapids=[4,4,5,5,3,4,6,14,1,11,8,7,12,16,20,21,19,24,1,2,2]
for line in audit.splitlines():
 if not re.match(r'^\| F\d{2} ',line): continue
 cells=[x.strip() for x in line.strip('|').split('|')]; no=int(cells[0][1:3]); k=knowledge[mapids[no-1]-1]
 disposition='EXCLUDED unsupported hardware/legal/authentication guarantees; retained limited masking condition' if no==20 else 'DISCLOSED provenance limitation; exclude unrelated/unseen content' if no==19 else 'RESOLVED by corrected/qualified curated claim'
 ledger+=f"## {cells[0]}\n\n**Affected objective:** {k['objective']}. **Original evidence:** {cells[1]}.\n\n**Problematic claim / historical technical issue:** {cells[2]}\n\n**Historical required treatment:** {cells[3]}\n\n**Selected interpretation:** {k['explanation']}\n\n**Mandatory qualifiers:** {k['conditions']}\n\n**Authoritative evidence:** {', '.join(k['references'])}, detailed locations in knowledge reference register. **Resolution/status:** {disposition}. **Confidence:** high for limited use. **Question impact:** {k['family']}; all items inherit these conditions; no unsupported related enrichment is assessed.\n\n"
ledger+='## Gate1 verdict\n\nPASS for authoring from the26 curated concepts: all9 High findings have a corrected foundation, no unresolved High affects a planned item, F07/F08 supplementation is present, and medium contradictions are qualified. F19 source-identity/cropping limits and F20 excluded enrichment remain nonblocking disclosures. No original source or historical audit file changed.\n'
Path('docs/plans/isc2-cc-2026/domain-5-production-remediation.md').write_text(ledger,encoding='utf-8')
terms=Path('docs/plans/isc2-cc-2026/domain-5-audit-terminology-matrix.md').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
termrows=[s for s in terms.splitlines() if s.startswith('| ') and len(s.split('|'))==11 and '5.' in s]
assert len(termrows)==51
termreport='# Domain 5 terminology revalidation and public-style research\n\nDate2026-11-03. All51 prior rows rechecked by the authoring assistant for spelling, abbreviation, technical meaning, Thai parity, parent objective, confused concepts and item use. No native-Thai/human approval is claimed. Original matrix remains unchanged. The new prompt contract uses existing real Thai translations as auxiliary fields, preserving English canonical text.\n\n## Public ISC2 style evidence\n\nR25 Knowledge Checks landing page was retrieved; its dynamic component did not expose actual CC sample questions to the text retrieval tool. No sample stem/option set was accessible in that retrieval and no claim of reviewing official sample wording is made. No third-party dumps were used. Concise stems, four parallel options and contextual FIRST/BEST are professional authoring recommendations from the audited rubric, not hidden ISC2 rules. Numbered scope verified at R01/R02.\n\n## Revalidation of all51 rows\n\n| Row | Canonical term | Disposition / usage | Curated evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n'
for i,row in enumerate(termrows,1):
 c=[x.strip() for x in row.strip('|').split('|')]; term=c[0]
 note='Retain corrected meaning; use only when relevant to the item decision; no separate jargon quota.'
 if 'Threat actor' in term: note='RESOLVED: CISA intentional/unintentional insider distinction; actor motivation explicitly stated; no attribution certainty.'
 if 'Quantum' in term: note='HOLD REMOVED: external PQC foundation now curated; no quantum hardware or implementation-math requirement.'
 if 'Data handling policy' in term: note='GAP FILLED: R21 RS.AN-06/07; supplied access/retention/sharing rules, no universal legal deadline.'
 if 'Password' in term: note='Retain explanation context only; dedicated salted costed password hashing, no extra password-policy recall item.'
 termreport+=f'| {i:02} | {term} | {note} Thai explanation checked against English conditions. | '+('R21/R23' if i in range(20,39) else 'R03/R04/R08/R19/R22' if i<20 else 'R11/R14/R20/R22')+'; see concept-specific26-record knowledge register |\n'
termreport+='\n## Decision-language controls\n\nBEST/MOST appropriate require decisive constraints; FIRST/INITIAL/NEXT require current stage, authority and prerequisites; PRIMARY/MAIN require one dominant purpose; MOST likely preserves uncertainty; LEAST is avoided unless needed. No phrase creates difficulty by itself. English/Thai qualifiers and option ordering must agree. Abbreviations are introduced where useful. Thai retains English security terms without silently expanding assurance.\n'
Path('docs/plans/isc2-cc-2026/domain-5-production-terminology.md').write_text(termreport,encoding='utf-8')
print('Gate1:26 curated foundations,21 remediation records,51 terminology revalidations; references',len(refs))
